Here is a sample code for a THREE.js slideshow with fade and zoom effects:
```
// Initialize the scene, camera, and renderer
const scene = new THREE.Scene();
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000);
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({
  canvas: document.getElementById('canvas'),
  antialias: true
});

// Set up the slides
const slides = [
  {
    texture: new THREE.TextureLoader().load('slide1.jpg'),
    scale: 1.5,
    duration: 3000
  },
  {
    texture: new THREE.TextureLoader().load('slide2.jpg'),
    scale: 2,
    duration: 2500
  },
  {
    texture: new THREE.TextureLoader().load('slide3.jpg'),
    scale: 1.8,
    duration: 3500
  }
];

// Create a plane to display the slides
const planeGeometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(10, 10);
const planeMaterial = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map: slides[0].texture });
const plane = new THREE.Mesh(planeGeometry, planeMaterial);
scene.add(plane);

// Animation variables
let currentSlideIndex = 0;
let animationStartTime = Date.now();

function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);

  // Calculate the time elapsed since the last animation frame
  const currentTime = Date.now();
  const timeElapsed = currentTime - animationStartTime;

  // Update the slide if the current one has finished playing
  if (timeElapsed > slides[currentSlideIndex].duration) {
    currentSlideIndex = (currentSlideIndex + 1) % slides.length;
    animationStartTime = currentTime;
  }

  // Fade out the previous slide
  const fadeOutProgress = Math.min(timeElapsed / slides[currentSlideIndex].duration, 1);
  plane.material.opacity = 1 - fadeOutProgress;

  // Zoom out the previous slide
  const zoomOutProgress = Math.min(timeElapsed / slides[currentSlideIndex].duration, 1);
  plane.scale.set(slides[currentSlideIndex].scale * (1 - zoomOutProgress), slides[currentSlideIndex].scale * (1 - zoomOutProgress), 1);

  // Fade in the next slide
  const fadeInProgress = Math.max((timeElapsed - slides[currentSlideIndex].duration) / slides[(currentSlideIndex + 1) % slides.length].duration, 0);
  plane.material.map = slides[(currentSlideIndex + 1) % slides.length].texture;
  plane.material.opacity = fadeInProgress;

  // Zoom in the next slide
  const zoomInProgress = Math.max((timeElapsed - slides[currentSlideIndex].duration) / slides[(currentSlideIndex + 1) % slides.length].duration, 0);
  plane.scale.set(slides[(currentSlideIndex + 1) % slides.length].scale * zoomInProgress, slides[(currentSlideIndex + 1) % slides.length].scale * zoomInProgress, 1);

  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}

animate();
```
This code assumes that you have three images named `slide1.jpg`, `slide2.jpg`, and `slide3.jpg` in the same directory as your HTML file. You'll need to adjust the `slides` array to match your specific image files and desired durations.

The animation works by fading out the previous slide while zooming it out, and then fading in the next slide while zooming it in. The `animate()` function is called repeatedly using `requestAnimationFrame()`, which updates the animation state based on the time elapsed since the last frame.

Note that this is just a basic example, and you may want to add additional features such as navigation controls or transitions between slides.
